Irreducible Counterfactuality and Query Complexity: An Information-Theoretic Sketch of Decision-Theoretic Imagination

Second draft — working paper

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

We begin from a naïve claim — that hallucination is a descriptor of supreme intelligence — and show that it survives no direct formalization. We extract a defensible core: counterfactuality, understood as the capacity to evaluate unrealized transitions, plays a measurable decision-theoretic role in certain environment classes. We propose a framework distinguishing (i) explicit hypothesis generation, (ii) complexity of the optimal policy, (iii) complexity of discovery, (iv) query complexity under observational versus counterfactual access, and (v) value of fallible simulation. We prove a deterministic separation: there exists a family \mathcal{M}_n for which the query gap between the two access modes is exponential, under identical per-interaction computational budgets. We identify three structural conditions — causal depth, failure opacity, and signal sparsity — each separately necessary for the gap. We conjecture, but do not prove, that their conjunction is sufficient under a formal independence condition on the agent. We introduce a distinct quantity \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} measuring the value of fallible counterfactual simulation, which is the correct formalization of the hallucination intuition. Results marked "Theorem" are proved or sketched; those marked "Conjecture" remain open.

Keywords. Counterfactuality, query complexity, reinforcement learning, generative model, causal inference, Kolmogorov complexity, lower bounds.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The initial intuition was:

A superior intelligence is characterized by heightened imagination, and hallucination is a descriptor of it.

This is too strong. It conflates three distinct objects:

\boxed{
\text{heightened imagination}
\;\neq\;
\text{clinical hallucination}
\;\neq\;
\text{supreme intelligence}
}

A hallucination, strictly, is a representation treated as perceptually real without corresponding stimulus. A superior intelligence must instead discriminate representations consistent with reality from those that are not. We therefore posit the distinction

G = \text{hypothesis generation}, \qquad V = \text{verification},

and observe that advanced intelligence corresponds rather to

G \uparrow, \quad V \uparrow

than to G \uparrow, V \downarrow.

1.2 Progressive reformulation

The intuition has been replaced by successively more precise quantities:

\text{imagination}
\to G
\to C_{\mathrm{cf}}
\to D_{\mathrm{causal}}
\to K_{\mathrm{irr}}
\to Q_{\mathrm{obs}}/Q_{\mathrm{cf}}
\to \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}.

Each step was obtained by destroying the preceding one. The present document fixes the last two steps as a research program.

1.3 Contributions

1. A formal framework distinguishing observational from counterfactual access (§2–3).
2. A query-gap measure \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} capturing the decision value of counterfactuality (§3).
3. A deterministic separation theorem \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}(\mathcal{M}_n) = \Omega(2^n) (§4).
4. Three structural conditions (causal depth, failure opacity, signal sparsity), each shown separately necessary (§5).
5. A distinct measure \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} for fallible simulation, formalizing the hallucination intuition (§6).
6. Falsifiable conjectures, including a weakened sufficiency conjecture conditioned on agent-class independence (§7).

1.4 Plan

Section 2 fixes notation. Section 3 defines query complexities. Section 4 proves the deterministic separation. Section 5 isolates necessary structural conditions. Section 6 introduces the fallible-simulation gap. Section 7 states conjectures. Section 8 discusses. Section 9 lists open problems.

