2. Formal Framework

2.1 MDPs and environment classes

An environment is a deterministic episodic MDP

M = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, P, r, s_0, T),

with state space \mathcal{S}, action space \mathcal{A}, transition P : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{S}, reward r : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathbb{R}, initial state s_0, horizon T. Stochastic extensions are treated in §8.1.

An environment class is denoted \mathcal{M}. For the main construction, \mathcal{M} = \mathcal{M}_n will be a finite class indexed by a hidden parameter.

2.2 Policies and regret

A policy is a map \pi : \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{A}. Its regret on M at horizon T is

R_T(\pi, M) = \sup_{\pi^*} \mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \gamma^t r(s_t, a_t)\right] - \mathbb{E}_\pi\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \gamma^t r(s_t, a_t)\right].

A policy is \varepsilon-optimal on \mathcal{M} if

\sup_{M \in \mathcal{M}} R_T(\pi, M) \le \varepsilon.

2.3 Two access modes

Observational access. At each step t, the agent observes only the quadruple

(s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1}),

and may act only in states actually reached.

Counterfactual access. In addition, the agent has a perfect oracle

O(s,a) = \bigl(P(s,a),\, r(s,a)\bigr)

for every (s,a) \in \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A}. This is the generative model of Kearns–Singh [1998].

Remark. The counterfactual agent is not "imaginative" in a psychological sense. It interrogates the world at unvisited points. Counterfactuality, here, is an interrogation capability, not a cognitive virtue. The distinction between a perfect oracle and an inferred model is taken up in §6.

2.4 Policy complexity versus discovery complexity

Define

K_{\mathrm{sol}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) = \min\{K(\pi) : \pi \text{ is } \varepsilon\text{-optimal on } \mathcal{M}\},

the description complexity of an optimal policy, and

K_{\mathrm{disc}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) = \min\{C(\mathcal{A}) : \operatorname{Regret}_T(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M}) \le \varepsilon\},

the complexity of a discovery algorithm.

Critical remark. Neither quantity measures counterfactuality:

· K_{\mathrm{sol}} may be O(1) while K_{\mathrm{disc}} is exponential (LQR: linear optimal policy, potentially difficult discovery).
· K_{\mathrm{disc}} may be large with no counterfactuality (random search in a bandit).

We therefore abandon K as the principal unit and pass to query complexity.

