3. Observational and Counterfactual Query Complexity

3.1 Definitions

Fix \varepsilon > 0. Define

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) = \min\{n : \exists\, \mathcal{A} \text{ observational reaching } \varepsilon\text{-optimality in } n \text{ interactions}\},

Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) = \min\{n : \exists\, \mathcal{A} \text{ with oracle reaching } \varepsilon\text{-optimality in } n \text{ queries}\}.

An interaction is a lived transition. A query is a call to O(s,a).

3.2 Counterfactual gap

Define the counterfactual query gap

\boxed{
\Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M})
=
Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) - Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}).
}

This measures the number of real interactions saved by counterfactual access:

· = 0 if observation suffices;
· > 0 if counterfactuality helps;
· possibly exponential if observation alone is prohibitive.

3.3 Budget model

We emphasize: the two agents share the same per-interaction budget

B_{\text{per-interaction}} = \operatorname{poly}(n),

not the same total budget. The gap is in the number of interactions, not in unit cost. This distinction is essential and is stated explicitly to avoid a common misreading of earlier drafts.

---

4. Exponential Separation (Deterministic Case)

4.1 Construction: the opaque combinatorial lock

Fix n \in \mathbb{N}. Define \mathcal{M}_n as follows.

States. \mathcal{S}_n = \{0, 1, \dots, n\} \cup \{\bot\}.

Actions. \mathcal{A} = \{0, 1\}.

Transition. For i \in \{0, \dots, n-1\}:

P(i, a) =
\begin{cases}
i+1 & \text{if } a = \pi^*(i), \\
\bot & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}

where \pi^* : \{0, \dots, n-1\} \to \{0,1\} is a hidden binary string. The state \bot is absorbing and opaque: it carries no information about the index i at which failure occurred, nor about \pi^*.

Reward. r(i, a) = 0 for i < n; r(n, \cdot) = 1.

Class. \mathcal{M}_n = \{M_{\pi^*} : \pi^* \in \{0,1\}^n\}.

Remark (opacity). Opacity of \bot is essential. If instead failures were distinguished by n distinct absorbing states \bot_0, \dots, \bot_{n-1} revealing the failure index, the observational agent could perform binary search under reset and achieve Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon = O(n). The exponential lower bound requires both opacity and the absence of cheap reset — but the two conditions are logically orthogonal.

4.2 Separation theorem

Theorem 1 (exponential separation). For every \varepsilon < 1,

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}_n) = \Omega(2^n),
\qquad
Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}_n) = O(n),

under identical per-interaction budget B = \operatorname{poly}(n).

Sketch of proof.

Observational lower bound. To learn \pi^*(i), the agent must reach state i and try both actions there. Every incorrect action terminates the episode in the opaque state \bot, yielding no signal about the failure index. Each episode thus supplies at most \log_2 n bits of information: the value of \pi^* at the first freshly-visited index, if any. By a counting argument, at least \Omega(2^n) episodes are needed in the worst case. Under reset between episodes, this bound is preserved. Absent reset, the bound is a fortiori preserved and can be strengthened to a lower bound on the number of distinct trajectories.

Counterfactual upper bound. An agent with oracle queries O(i, 0) and O(i, 1) for each i \in \{0, \dots, n-1\}. Exactly one of the two responses leads to i+1, the other to \bot. In 2n queries, \pi^* is fully determined. Value iteration on the resulting MDP yields an optimal policy in O(n) additional queries. Hence Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}_n) = O(n). \square

Corollary 1. For the family \{\mathcal{M}_n\}_{n \ge 1},

\Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}_n) = \Omega(2^n) - O(n) \to \infty.

4.3 On the nature of the separation

The gap is exponential under equal per-interaction budget. It is not a computational artifact. The advantage of the counterfactual agent is purely informational: access to unrealized transitions.

The construction is a variant of the combinatorial lock (Kearns–Singh [1998], Kakade [2003]). We claim no technical originality; the framing of the lock as a counterfactuality phenomenon is what is new here.

---

5. Necessary Structural Conditions

The construction of §4 satisfies three properties. We show each is separately necessary.

5.1 Causal depth

(i) Causal depth. The transition chain to reward has length n. Define

D_{\mathrm{causal}}(M) = \min\{d : \exists \pi, \pi' \text{ agreeing on first } d \text{ steps} \Rightarrow |R_T(\pi, M) - R_T(\pi', M)| < \varepsilon\}.

In \mathcal{M}_n, D_{\mathrm{causal}} = n.

5.2 Failure opacity

(ii) Failure opacity. The agent cannot distinguish a posteriori between distinct causes of failure.

Formally: the absorbing failure state is a single state \bot with no auxiliary observation revealing the index of failure. Equivalently, the map i \mapsto \bot is non-injective from the agent's observational perspective.

5.3 Signal sparsity

(iii) Signal sparsity. Reward is non-zero only at the terminal state n. No intermediate signal guides exploration.

5.4 Separate necessity

Proposition 2. Removing any one of (i), (ii), (iii) from \mathcal{M}_n yields Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}_n) = \operatorname{poly}(n).

· Remove (i): if D_{\mathrm{causal}} = O(1), the observational agent reaches reward in constant time.
· Remove (ii): with transparent failures revealing the index, binary search under reset yields O(n) episodes.
· Remove (iii): with dense reward, gradient ascent on cumulative reward yields O(n) interactions.

Proof. Immediate in each case. \square

Remark. Proposition 2 establishes separate necessity, not joint sufficiency. Joint sufficiency is Conjecture 1 (§7.1), weakened there to account for agent-class independence.

