6. Fallible Simulation: The Hallucination Gap

6.1 Distinguishing oracle from model

The oracle O(s,a) = (P(s,a), r(s,a)) of §2.3 is perfect. Counterfactual reasoning in real systems is not: an agent that simulates futures uses an inferred model \hat{M}, typically misspecified.

This distinction is essential. A perfect oracle cannot hallucinate — it merely interrogates. Hallucination requires a model whose output may deviate from reality. The correct formalization of the hallucination intuition is therefore not \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} but a distinct quantity.

6.2 Definition

Fix an agent \mathcal{A} with inferred model \hat{M} and simulation budget B_{\mathrm{sim}}. Define the hallucination gap

\boxed{
\Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M})
=
Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M})
-
Q_{\mathrm{sim}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \hat{M}, \mathcal{M}),
}

where Q_{\mathrm{sim}}^\varepsilon is the number of real interactions required when the agent may additionally simulate using \hat{M} subject to B_{\mathrm{sim}}.

This measure is signed:

· \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} > 0: simulation with \hat{M} is decision-useful.
· \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} < 0: simulation with \hat{M} is harmful (hallucination penalty).
· \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} = 0: simulation is decision-irrelevant.

6.3 Relation to counterfactuality

We have, schematically,

\Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} \ge \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}},

with equality iff \hat{M} = M (perfect model). The gap \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} - \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} quantifies the cost of model error in counterfactual use. It is the natural quantity in which to formalize the intuition that a superior intelligence simulates accurately:

\boxed{
\text{Superior intelligence} \approx \text{large } \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} \text{ with small } \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} - \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}.
}

This is a falsifiable statement, unlike the original intuition.

