7. Conjectures

7.1 Joint sufficiency (weakened)

The naïve form of sufficiency — that (i)+(ii)+(iii) imply exponential Q_{\mathrm{obs}} — is false. Counterexample. Adjoin to \mathcal{M}_n a compact generator G of size O(\log n) that outputs \pi^*(M) in clear for every M \in \mathcal{M}_n. The observational agent reads \pi^* directly, so Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon = O(n), while (i), (ii), (iii) still hold.

The correct statement requires agent-class independence:

Conjecture 1'. Let \mathcal{M} satisfy (i), (ii), (iii) with parameter n, and let \mathcal{A} be an agent whose description is independent of the specification of the hidden structure. Then for some c > 0,

> Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M}) \ge 2^{cn}.
> 

Status. The phrase description independent of the specification resists clean formalization without Kolmogorov paradoxes. This is an open problem (§9.2).

7.2 Genericity

Conjecture 2 (genericity). Let \mathcal{M} be an open class of deterministic, non-ergodic environments with growing horizon. Then generically,

> \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) \to \infty.
> 

"Generically" must be made precise: a natural measure on MDP classes, or a Baire category statement. The conjecture is likely false as stated and requires a measurable weakening. Status: open, ambitious.

7.3 Residual non-compressibility

Conjecture 3. \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) is exponential iff there exists a causally non-compressible structure:

> \inf_{\phi \in \Phi} I(\pi^*; \phi) \to \infty,
> 

where \Phi is the set of off-policy identifiable representations from polynomial samples.

This links counterfactuality to off-policy identifiability (Pearl [2009], Precup [2000]). Status: open.

7.4 Hallucination gap positivity

Conjecture 4. There exists a class \mathcal{M} and an inferred-model family \{\hat{M}_\theta\} such that

> \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M}) \to \infty
> 

with \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} - \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} bounded by a constant.

That is: fallible simulation is asymptotically as valuable as perfect simulation, in some class. Status: open, and likely the sharpest formalization of the original intuition.

