8. Discussion

8.1 The stochastic case is central, not peripheral

The construction of §4 is deterministic. In stochastic regimes:

· The observational agent can estimate probabilities by repeated sampling.
· The counterfactual agent can estimate P(\cdot \mid s,a) via oracle queries.
· The comparison changes qualitatively.

We now state: the deterministic case is degenerate. Stochastic ergodic environments destroy the gap. Stochastic non-ergodic environments with structured randomness may preserve it. The stochastic case is the heart of the program, not an appendix.

8.2 Return to the initial intuition

The intuition "hallucination is a descriptor of supreme intelligence" survives no direct formalization. What survives is:

Informal proposition. Certain environment classes force any \varepsilon-optimal policy to evaluate a large number of unrealized transitions, when realized transitions are rare, opaque on failure, or non-resettable.

This proposition is:

· falsifiable (one construction suffices to destroy it);
· non-trivial (it links a property of the world to an informational constraint on the agent);
· non-psychological (it says nothing about imagination as a faculty, only about counterfactuality as an informational access mode).

8.3 Counterfactuality \neq hallucination

The framework makes the distinction rigorous:

· A hallucination is a representation with no corresponding stimulus.
· A counterfactual is an evaluation of an unrealized transition consistent with a model.

Formally, a hallucination is a query O(s,a) whose output does not correspond to any real transition. Counterfactuality, by definition, is consistent with M. Under a perfect oracle, hallucination is impossible. Under an inferred model, hallucination is possible, and is measured by \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} - \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} in §6.3.

Hallucination is not a limiting case of counterfactuality. It is its failure mode.

8.4 Counterfactuality \neq universal necessity

We reject:

Superior intelligence requires high counterfactuality.

This is false. In ergodic, resettable, densely-signaled environments, a purely observational agent achieves optimality in polynomial time. Counterfactuality is not a universal virtue of intelligence; it is a response to specific environmental constraints.

Correct formulation:

\boxed{
\text{Counterfactuality is necessary exactly where the world refuses to be observed twice.}
}

"Refusing to be observed twice" is now formally articulated as: opaque failure, prohibitive reset cost, non-dense signal.

