9. Open Problems

9.1 Stochastic locks

What becomes of \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} under stochastic transitions? Ergodicity likely destroys the gap; latent-variable structure may preserve it. Priority: high.

9.2 Agent-class independence

Formalize the "independence of specification" required by Conjecture 1' without collapsing into Kolmogorov paradox. Candidates: conditional Kolmogorov complexity K(\mathcal{A} \mid \mathcal{M}), algorithmic independence à la Chaitin, information-theoretic independence under a reference prior. Priority: high.

9.3 Partial oracles

Our counterfactual agent has a total oracle. What of oracles available only on a subset of \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A}, noisy oracles, or cost-bounded oracles? A theory of the value of partial counterfactual access is to be built. Priority: medium.

9.4 Relation to causal inference

Establish a theorem linking \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} to causal identifiability in Pearl's sense. Under what conditions on the causal graph of M is the gap non-zero? Priority: medium.

9.5 Multi-agent counterfactuality

Counterfactuality includes "what would the other do if I did X?" — absent from our framework. Any theory of social intelligence requires this. Priority: medium.

9.6 Hierarchical intelligence

Our framework treats \varepsilon-optimality on a single class \mathcal{M}. It does not treat an agent's capacity to operate across a hierarchy \mathcal{M}_1 \subset \mathcal{M}_2 \subset \cdots. A theory of hierarchical counterfactuality is required to speak of general intelligence in the strong sense. Priority: highest.

9.7 Hallucination gap

Develop the theory of \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}. Under what conditions is fallible simulation asymptotically as valuable as perfect simulation? This is Conjecture 4 and is the sharpest remaining formalization of the original intuition. Priority: highest.

---

10. Conclusion

We began with a phrase that was too strong, and by a sequence of controlled destructions arrived at a testable formal framework:

\boxed{
\Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M})
=
Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M}) - Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{M})
}

as a measure of the decision value of counterfactuality, complemented by

\boxed{
\Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M})
=
Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \mathcal{M}) - Q_{\mathrm{sim}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal{A}, \hat{M}, \mathcal{M})
}

as a measure of the decision value of fallible simulation.

We have proved an exponential separation in the deterministic case under equal per-interaction budget, identified three separately necessary structural conditions (causal depth, failure opacity, signal sparsity), and formulated falsifiable conjectures on their joint sufficiency (Conjecture 1'), genericity (Conjecture 2), residual non-compressibility (Conjecture 3), and the hallucination gap (Conjecture 4).

This document contains no deep theorem. It contains a map: a map of where philosophical debate about imagination must yield to lower bounds in decision theory under informational constraints. That map alone justifies abandoning the initial phrase — not because it is false, but because it is no longer precise enough to be interesting.

The next object to attack is \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}. That is where the original intuition, for the first time, becomes falsifiable.

