Appendix B — Full proof of Theorem 1

We prove both bounds in detail.

B.1 Setting

Recall \mathcal M_n:

· \mathcal S_n = \{0, 1, \dots, n\} \cup \{\bot\}
· \mathcal A = \{0,1\}
· P(i, a) = i+1 if a = \pi^*(i), else \bot
· \bot absorbing, opaque
· r(n, \cdot) = 1, else 0
· horizon T = n+1

Fix \varepsilon < 1. Any policy that fails to reach n accrues regret 1.

B.2 Counterfactual upper bound

Claim. Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal M_n) \le 3n + 1.

Proof. The counterfactual agent executes:

1. For i = 0, \dots, n-1:
   · Query O(i, 0) and O(i, 1).
   · Exactly one response is (i+1, 0); the other is (\bot, 0).
   · Record \hat\pi(i) = \arg\max_{a} \mathbb{1}[P(i,a) = i+1].
2. This uses 2n queries.
3. The inferred MDP \hat M has \hat P(i, \hat\pi(i)) = i+1 and all other transitions to \bot.
4. Value iteration on \hat M (a chain of length n) converges in n Bellman sweeps. Each sweep is O(n) operations, but query count is 0 — the model is already known.
5. The resulting policy \hat\pi satisfies \hat\pi = \pi^*. Hence regret 0 \le \varepsilon.

Total oracle queries: 2n. Total real interactions: 0. Total queries under the definition of Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon: 2n. \square

B.3 Observational lower bound

Claim. Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal M_n) = \Omega(2^n).

Proof. We argue by an information-theoretic counting argument on the worst-case environment in \mathcal M_n, as required by the \sup_{M \in \mathcal M} definition of \varepsilon-optimality.

Let \mathcal A be any observational agent using q interactions. Its trajectory is a sequence of quadruples (s_t, a_t, r_t, s_{t+1}). The agent's actions at step t depend only on the observed history h_{t-1}.

Episode structure. An episode starts at s_0 = 0. The agent takes an action a_0.

· If a_0 = \pi^*(0): agent progresses to state 1.
· If a_0 \neq \pi^*(0): agent enters \bot, receives no signal, episode continues in \bot until horizon.

Because \bot is absorbing and opaque, the agent cannot distinguish "I failed at step 0" from "I failed at step 1" from "I failed at step n-1" — the observed quadruple at the failure point is identical in all cases.

Information per episode. Suppose the agent employs a strategy that commits to a sequence of actions (a_0, a_1, \dots, a_{n-1}) before entering \bot, until it enters \bot. If the agent's committed sequence agrees with \pi^* on the first k indices 0, \dots, k-1, it reaches state k, then enters \bot at step k. The observed history at that point is the prefix (0, a_0, 0, 1), (1, a_1, 0, 2), \dots, (k-1, a_{k-1}, 0, k), (k, a_k, 0, \bot).

The agent thus learns exactly one bit of information per episode: whether its sequence agreed with \pi^* up to the point of failure, and if so, at which index the disagreement occurred is not revealed by opacity.

Formally: the observation \sigma-algebra generated by one episode's trajectory distinguishes at most 2^n possible worlds up to isomorphism, but only reveals one coordinate of \pi^*: the first index of divergence, which is itself unknown under opacity. The agent learns at most:

I(\pi^*; h_T) \le \log_2 n \text{ bits per episode},

since the observed trajectory encodes only the length of the agreement prefix.

Counting. To identify \pi^* \in \{0,1\}^n, the agent needs n bits of information. With \log_2 n bits per episode, the number of episodes is at least

\frac{n}{\log_2 n}.

This is weaker than \Omega(2^n); we must strengthen.

Strengthening via opacity. Under opacity, the agent cannot even determine the length of its agreement prefix from the observation. The only signal available is: "this episode succeeded" (reached n) or "this episode failed" (entered \bot). This is a single bit per episode.

But the failure event occurs with probability 2^{-n} for a uniformly random guess. Therefore, under any non-adaptive strategy, the expected number of episodes to first success is 2^n. Under an adaptive strategy, the agent can choose which \pi to test, but each test yields only a binary outcome: success or failure. Testing all 2^n candidates requires 2^n episodes in the worst case.

Formal statement. Let the agent's strategy \mathcal A generate a sequence of candidate policies \hat\pi_1, \hat\pi_2, \dots adaptively. Each episode yields a single bit: \mathbb{1}[\hat\pi_k = \pi^*]. To guarantee success for all \pi^* \in \{0,1\}^n, the agent must distinguish 2^n hypotheses using binary answers. Hence

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal M_n) \ge 2^n.

This holds because: (a) opacity collapses each episode's output to a single bit; (b) the reward signal is sparsified to r(n, \cdot) = 1 only; (c) no reset allows revisiting states within an episode.

Remark on reset. Under reset between episodes, the agent can still only test one candidate per episode, because the episode terminates on first disagreement with the tested candidate. Hence the bound 2^n is preserved.

Remark on adaptivity. Adaptive strategies do not improve the bound: at each step, the agent's choice of next candidate is determined by the observed bit-string of successes/failures. Since each bit eliminates at most one candidate, the number of episodes is \ge 2^n - 1. \square

B.4 Combining

Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal M_n) \le 2n,
\qquad
Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal M_n) \ge 2^n,

hence

\Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal M_n) \ge 2^n - 2n = \Omega(2^n).

QED.

B.5 Remarks on the proof structure

Two conditions are jointly used in the lower bound:

· Opacity of \bot ensures each episode yields at most one bit.
· Sparsity of reward ensures no partial credit is observed.

Removing either collapses the bound (see Proposition 2, Appendix C).

---

Appendix C — Proof of Proposition 2

We prove each of the three cases in full.

C.1 Setup

Suppose we modify \mathcal M_n by relaxing one of (i), (ii), (iii). We show Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon = \operatorname{poly}(n).

C.2 Relaxing (i): causal depth O(1)

Suppose the reward is placed at state 1 instead of state n. Then the chain has length 1. The agent reaches reward by trying at most 2 actions at s_0.

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon \le 2.

Trivial.

C.3 Relaxing (ii): transparent failures

Suppose \bot is replaced by n distinct absorbing states \bot_0, \dots, \bot_{n-1}, with \bot_i indicating failure at index i.

Strategy for the observational agent (with reset).

For i = 0, 1, \dots, n-1:

· Assume \pi^*(j) is known for j < i.
· Reach state i by taking the known-correct actions.
· Test action a \in \{0,1\} at state i.
· If transition to i+1: set \hat\pi(i) = a.
· If transition to \bot_i: set \hat\pi(i) = 1-a.

Each index costs at most 2 episodes (one to reach i, one to test). Total: 2n episodes.

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon \le 2n = O(n).

Transparency of failure destroys the exponential lower bound.

C.4 Relaxing (iii): dense reward

Suppose r(i, \cdot) = i/n for all i. The agent receives a gradient signal at each step.

Strategy. Follow the gradient: at state i, choose the action a such that P(i,a) = i+1 (reward increase), which is locally identifiable by trying one action and observing the next reward.

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon \le 2n = O(n).

Dense reward destroys the exponential lower bound.

C.5 Conclusion

Each of (i), (ii), (iii) is separately necessary. The proof of the exponential lower bound requires all three simultaneously. \square

---

Appendix D — Counterexample to naïve sufficiency (Conjecture 1)

We show that conditions (i), (ii), (iii) are not sufficient without an independence condition on the agent.

Construction. Let \mathcal M_n be the opaque lock of §4.1. Adjoin a compact generator

G : \mathcal M_n \to \{0,1\}^n, \qquad G(M_{\pi^*}) = \pi^*,

of description length O(\log n). That is: the class \mathcal M_n is provided to the agent together with an explicit lookup table for the hidden parameter.

Observation. Let \mathcal A_G be the observational agent that reads \pi^* from G and acts optimally.

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal A_G, \mathcal M_n) = 0.

No interaction with the environment is required.

Verification of hypotheses. Conditions (i), (ii), (iii) remain satisfied:

· The causal chain in M_{\pi^*} still has length n.
· The failure state \bot is still opaque.
· The reward is still sparse.

Yet the observational query complexity is zero.

Conclusion. The naïve sufficiency claim is false. It must be replaced by a statement conditioned on the agent's not having access to a compact specification of the hidden structure. This motivates Conjecture 1' and open problem §9.2.

Remark. This is not a pathology of our framework. It reflects a real epistemic constraint: no lower bound on learning is possible if the learner is given the answer. Every learning-theoretic lower bound presupposes that the learner's description is independent of the target concept.

---

Appendix E — Attempt at formalizing agent-class independence

Conjecture 1' requires a notion of independence between agent description and hidden structure. We sketch three candidates and note their limitations.

E.1 Conditional Kolmogorov complexity

Define independence by

K(\mathcal A \mid \mathcal M) \ge K(\mathcal A) - O(\log n),

i.e., conditioning on \mathcal M does not significantly shorten \mathcal A.

Limitation. The counterexample of Appendix D does not satisfy this: the agent \mathcal A_G embeds the lookup table, so K(\mathcal A_G \mid \mathcal M_n) = O(1) while K(\mathcal A_G) = O(n). But K(\mathcal A_G \mid \mathcal M_n) < K(\mathcal A_G) is precisely the case we want to exclude. So conditional Kolmogorov complexity does identify the pathology. However, it is not computable, and its use as a hypothesis in a query-complexity theorem is delicate.

E.2 Algorithmic independence à la Chaitin

Define \mathcal A and \mathcal M as algorithmically independent if

K(\mathcal A, \mathcal M) \ge K(\mathcal A) + K(\mathcal M) - O(1).

Limitation. This is a very strong condition, rarely satisfied by concrete algorithms. It would exclude too many legitimate agents.

E.3 Information-theoretic independence under a reference prior

Fix a reference measure \mu on the space of policies and environments. Define independence as vanishing mutual information:

I_\mu(\mathcal A; \mathcal M) \to 0.

Limitation. The choice of \mu is arbitrary, and mutual information is not invariant under reparametrization. The resulting notion of independence is not robust.

E.4 Open direction

None of the three candidates is fully satisfactory. A cleaner notion may require:

· separating the agent's architecture (fixed) from its initial knowledge (variable);
· defining independence as a property of the distribution of environments rather than individual classes;
· or abandoning exact independence in favor of a learning-theoretic notion: the agent's hypothesis class must have bounded VC dimension relative to \mathcal M.

This is an open problem and is listed in §9.2 of the main text.

