Appendix F — Methodological note: purely theoretical work

F.1 Statement of scope

This document is a theoretical contribution. It contains:

· definitions;
· constructions of environment classes;
· proofs and proof sketches of lower and upper bounds;
· conjectures with proposed falsification criteria.

It does not contain:

· empirical experiments;
· computational simulations;
· training of any machine learning model;
· benchmark evaluations;
· code implementations.

F.2 Why this is appropriate

The central objects of the paper — Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon, Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon, \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}}^\varepsilon, \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}}^\varepsilon — are asymptotic query complexities. They are defined as infima over the set of all algorithms reaching a criterion. They are not quantities that can be estimated by running a finite number of experiments.

For example, the claim

Q_{\mathrm{obs}}^\varepsilon(\mathcal M_n) = \Omega(2^n)

is a statement about the behavior of all possible observational agents as n \to \infty. No finite experiment can establish or refute it.

F.3 What an empirical program would look like

If one wanted to complement this theory with empirics, the natural direction would be:

1. Instantiate \mathcal M_n for small n (e.g., n \le 20).
2. Implement a concrete observational agent (Q-learning, R-max, posterior sampling).
3. Implement a concrete counterfactual agent (model-based RL with oracle).
4. Measure the empirical ratio \hat Q_{\mathrm{obs}} / \hat Q_{\mathrm{cf}} as a function of n.
5. Compare against the theoretical prediction \Omega(2^n / n).

This would provide illustrative evidence — not proof. The theory stands or falls on the proofs and constructions, not on empirical agreement.

Such an empirical program is not carried out here. It is explicitly listed as future work.

F.4 Epistemic hygiene

Given the absence of empirical work, the following claims must be treated as:

· Proved in this document: Theorem 1 (Appendix B), Proposition 2 (Appendix C), counterexample of Appendix D.
· Conjectural: Conjectures 1'–4 of §7.
· Speculative: discussions of §8.
· Programmatic: open problems of §9.

The reader is invited to distinguish these registers carefully. In particular, the conjecture that "generic environments make counterfactuality necessary" (Conjecture 2) has no proof and rests on no empirical evidence. It is a target, not a result.

