Appendix H — Relation to existing literature

We situate the paper precisely.

H.1 Generative model RL

Kearns–Singh [1998] introduced the model-based access assumption we adopt. Their results are about polynomial-time near-optimal RL given a generative model. We do not challenge their results; we use their access model to define Q_{\mathrm{cf}} and contrast it with Q_{\mathrm{obs}}.

Difference. They ask: what is learnable with a generative model? We ask: how much does the generative model save in interactions, as a function of the environment's structural properties?

H.2 Combinatorial locks and hard-exploration

The construction of §4.1 is a variant of hard-exploration benchmarks well known in RL (e.g., delayed reward, sparse reward, no reset). Our contribution is not the construction but its framing: we identify opacity of failure as a separately necessary condition, orthogonal to reset and reward sparsity.

H.3 Off-policy evaluation

Precup [2000] and the off-policy literature study the estimation of a target policy's value from data generated by a different policy. Our Q_{\mathrm{cf}} is a query-complexity analogue: how many queries to a model suffice to evaluate all policies? The connection to identifiability (Conjecture 3) is natural but not yet formalized.

H.4 Causal inference

Pearl [2009] distinguishes observational, interventional, and counterfactual queries. Our framework operates at the interventional level (querying P(s' \mid s, a)), not at the counterfactual level in Pearl's strict sense (which requires a structural causal model and abduction). Our use of "counterfactual" is therefore looser than Pearl's. This should be flagged. A fully Pearl-correct version would require augmenting the MDP with a structural causal model.

H.5 Kolmogorov complexity in RL

Hutter [2005] uses Kolmogorov complexity to define universal intelligence. We use it in §2.4 only to reject it as the primary unit, and in Appendix E only as a candidate formalization of independence. We do not build a universal intelligence measure.

H.6 What is new

To our knowledge, the following are new in framing (not in technical content):

1. The distinction between causal depth, failure opacity, and signal sparsity as separately necessary conditions (§5).
2. The definition of \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} as the correct formalization of the hallucination intuition (§6).
3. The identification of the agent-class independence problem (Appendix E, §9.2) as the central technical obstacle to a sufficiency theorem.

