Appendix I — Working notes and acknowledged gaps

For transparency, we list the known weaknesses of this draft.

I.1 Proof completeness

· Theorem 1 (Appendix B) is proved but the opacity-based information argument in B.3 could be tightened. A fully rigorous treatment would model the agent as a decision tree and bound its depth by counting leaves.
· Proposition 2 (Appendix C) is proved in all three cases, but the interaction between the three conditions is not analyzed. Whether (i)+(ii)+(iii) have independent contributions or are entangled is open.

I.2 Conjectural content

· Conjecture 1' depends on an unformalized independence condition (Appendix E).
· Conjecture 2 ("genericity") has no measure specified. It should be treated as a research direction, not a claim.
· Conjecture 3 relies on I(\pi^*; \phi) which mixes Kolmogorov and Shannon notions; this is not rigorous.
· Conjecture 4 (\Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} positivity) is new and unproved. It may be false.

I.3 Missing cases

· The stochastic case is acknowledged as central (§8.1) but not treated.
· The multi-agent case is acknowledged (§9.5) but not treated.
· The hierarchical case (§9.6) is acknowledged as most important but is entirely absent from the technical content.

I.4 Methodological honesty

· No experiments, no simulations, no code.
· All numerical-looking quantities are asymptotic.
· All conjectures are explicitly labeled.

I.5 What would falsify the paper

· A construction of a class satisfying (i), (ii), (iii) with Q_{\mathrm{obs}} = \operatorname{poly}(n) and satisfying a reasonable independence condition would kill Conjecture 1'.
· A proof that \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} is always bounded by \Delta Q_{\mathrm{cf}} up to constants would kill Conjecture 4.
· A demonstration that "counterfactual" in our sense is reducible to Pearl's interventional queries without loss would trivialize part of §6.

I.6 Next actions (for the author)

1. Complete the proof of Theorem 1 with a decision-tree formalism.
2. Attack Conjecture 1' by searching for a second counterexample beyond Appendix D.
3. Formalize the stochastic lock and determine whether the gap persists.
4. Define \Delta Q_{\mathrm{hall}} rigorously with a model-error term.
5. Investigate the hierarchical case, which is the true target of the original intuition.

